---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FederalRanking from '../../components/FederalRanking.astro';
import federalData from '../../data/federal-ranking.json';
import AiSummary from '../../components/AiSummary.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { rankingGroups, levels, specialties, modifiers } from '../../data/site';
import { rankingCopy } from '../../data/ranking-copy';
import { stats, indexFactors } from '../../data/quality-index';
import { qualityStudy } from '../../data/research';

const total = levels.length + specialties.length + modifiers.length;

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'How many nutrition programs do you rank?',
    answer: `Our database holds ${stats.programs} programs at ${stats.schools} institutions across ${stats.states} states, each linked to its official program page. Every ranking on this page is generated from that database rather than compiled by hand, which is why the same program can appear in several rankings and always carries the same score.`
  },
  {
    question: 'Do institutions pay to appear in these rankings?',
    answer:
      'No. We do not accept payment for inclusion, position, or removal, and we have no affiliate relationships with any institution listed. If that changes we will say so on this page before it appears anywhere else.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does one program appear in several rankings?',
    answer:
      'Because a program can be a bachelor degree, in dietetics, available online, and at a public institution all at once. The rankings are views onto one database rather than separate lists, so a program that fits four filters appears in four rankings with an identical score.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I choose a program based on its ranking position?',
    answer:
      'No, and we would rather say that plainly than pretend our score is more useful than it is. Use the ranking to build a shortlist, then decide on three things the index cannot see: net cost after aid for you specifically, whether the department arranges supervised practice in the region you want to work in, and whether the accreditation status is current. Those three beat any position on any list.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between a level ranking and a specialty ranking?',
    answer:
      'A level ranking answers what credential you are pursuing, from certificate through doctorate. A specialty ranking answers what part of the field you want to work in, from dietetics through food service management. Most people should read one of each, because those are two independent decisions and confusing them is a common planning error.'
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Nutrition Program Rankings"
  description={`All ${total} nutrition and dietetics program rankings by degree level, specialty, and format, scored with the published seven-factor Program Quality Index across ${stats.programs} programs.`}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Rankings</p>
      <h1>Nutrition program rankings, scored on published evidence</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {total} rankings generated from {stats.programs} programs at {stats.schools} institutions. Every score comes
        from the seven-factor {qualityStudy.shortName}, every factor is published, and no institution pays to be here.
      </p>
      <div class="metrics">
        <div><strong>{stats.programs}</strong><span>programs scored</span></div>
        <div><strong>{stats.accredited}</strong><span>institutions with an ACEND program</span></div>
        <div><strong>{stats.withPractice}</strong><span>documenting supervised practice</span></div>
        <div><strong>{stats.online}</strong><span>with an online option</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <AiSummary>
        NutritionSchools.org publishes {total} rankings of nutrition and dietetics programs, organised by degree level
        (certificate, associate, bachelor, master, doctorate), by specialty (dietetics, sports nutrition, public health
        nutrition, clinical nutrition, nutritional science, food service management), and by format and cost (online,
        affordable, and programs documenting supervised practice). All are generated from a database of
        {stats.programs} programs at {stats.schools} institutions across {stats.states} states, each linked to its
        official program page. Scores come from the {qualityStudy.name}, a seven-factor index distributing 100 points
        across curriculum rigor (18), supervised practice (17), faculty credentials (15), program accreditation (15),
        clinical and community partnerships (15), career outcomes (10), and cost value (10). Every factor is computed
        from published institutional or accreditation data, so any score can be reconstructed. We do not use reputation
        surveys, we do not accept payment for placement, and we publish the six things the index cannot measure. Last
        reviewed August 2026.
      </AiSummary>

      <div class="hot-take">
        <strong>Read this before the lists</strong>
        <p>
          Most nutrition rankings are built on reputation surveys, which measure name recognition among people who have
          never taught at the schools they are rating. Ours measures documented program attributes. That makes it
          checkable, which also makes it arguable, and we would rather you argue with a specific factor weight than
          trust us.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="hot-take">
        <strong>Geography still beats a ranking slot</strong>
        <p>
          A national list is a shortlist, not a decision. Compare the program pages in
          {' '}<a href="/states/california/">California</a> and
          {' '}<a href="/states/texas/">Texas</a> against a thin coverage page like
          {' '}<a href="/states/south-carolina/">South Carolina</a>, then use the
          {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/states/">full state directory</a> to see whether supervised practice in
          your region is even available.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {rankingGroups.map(group => (
    <section class="section alt">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">{group.label}</p>
            <h2>{group.items.length} rankings</h2>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid">
          {group.items.map(item => (
            <a class="card" href={`/rankings/${item.slug}/`}>
              <span class="label">{group.label}</span>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p>{item.intro}</p>
              <span class="text-link">{rankingCopy[item.slug]?.cardCta ?? 'Open this ranking →'}</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <article class="content wrap">
    <h2>How the Program Quality Index works</h2>
    <p>
      Seven factors, 100 points. Each one is computed from something an institution or the Accreditation Council for
      Education in Nutrition and Dietetics publishes, which means you can reconstruct any score we show from the
      public record and tell us where we got the weighting wrong.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Weight</th><th>What it measures</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {indexFactors.map(([name, weight, description]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{name}</strong></td><td>{weight}</td><td>{description}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What these rankings deliberately do not measure</h2>
    <ul>{qualityStudy.excludes.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
    <p>
      That list is the most useful part of any ranking methodology and it is the part almost nobody publishes. A score
      that hides its blind spots is asking for trust. A score that names them is asking to be checked, which is a
      better deal for you. The <a class="text-link" href="/methodology/">full methodology</a> goes further into how
      each factor is computed.
    </p>

    <h2>How to actually use a ranking</h2>
    <p>
      Build a shortlist of eight to twelve programs from the relevant level and specialty lists. Then leave the
      ranking behind entirely and do four things.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Verify ACEND accreditation for the specific program with ACEND directly, and check whether the status is full accreditation or candidacy. Program pages are not always current.</li>
      <li>Ask who arranges supervised practice, at which named sites, and what happens when a placement falls through. Get the answer in writing.</li>
      <li>Get a net cost figure from the financial aid office for your circumstances, not the published sticker price.</li>
      <li>Read the required course list. If biochemistry and medical nutrition therapy are not in it, the program is not preparing you for the jobs in this field.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A program at position fourteen that does all four of those things well is a better choice than a program at
      position two that does not. We build the lists so you can stop using them sooner.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the 2024 graduate-degree rule is why you are here,
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/rankings/best-nutrition-masters-programs/">compare nutrition master's programs</a>
      that fold supervised practice into the degree. If you want the RDN gate itself, start with
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/rankings/best-acend-accredited-nutrition-programs/">ACEND accredited dietetics pathways</a>.
      Then read the <a class="text-link" href="/careers/registered-dietitian-nutritionist/">RDN career guide</a> and the
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/methodology/">full scoring method</a>.
    </p>

    <Faq items={faq} />

    <div class="notice">
      <strong>Verify before you decide:</strong> accreditation status, tuition, curriculum, and supervised practice
      arrangements change. Every listing links to the institution's own page, which is the authoritative source. Our
      database covers {stats.programs} programs and is not exhaustive.
    </div>
  </article>
<FederalRanking data={federalData} heading="Best Nutrition and Dietetics Bachelor’s Programs, Ranked on Federal Data" /></BaseLayout>
